---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import type { Entry } from '../lib/derive';
import { siteData } from '../lib/load';
import { href, md, wbr } from '../lib/util';

const { entries, stats, sigmaOrphans } = siteData();
const byFlag = (f: string) => entries.filter((e) => e.flags.includes(f));
const drift = entries.filter((e) => e.flags.includes('drift') || e.flags.includes('case-drift'));
const noOfn = byFlag('no-ofn');
const dllNamed = byFlag('dll-ofn');
const differs = byFlag('differs-from-name')
  .filter((e) => !e.flags.includes('dll-ofn'))
  .sort((a, b) => b.sigma_rule_refs - a.sigma_rule_refs || a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const driftGotcha = (e: Entry) => e.gotchas.find((g) => g.kind === 'drift' || g.kind === 'case-drift')?.text ?? '';
const dllOf = (e: Entry) => e.distinct_values.find((v) => v.toLowerCase().endsWith('.dll')) ?? '';

// Observed on Windows 11 build 26300: Explorer / (Get-Item x).VersionInfo vs. raw PE resource (= Sysmon)
const mui = [
  ['cmd.exe', 'Cmd.Exe.MUI', 'Cmd.Exe'],
  ['powershell.exe', 'PowerShell.EXE.MUI', 'PowerShell.EXE'],
  ['rundll32.exe', 'RUNDLL32.EXE.MUI', 'RUNDLL32.EXE'],
  ['certutil.exe', 'CertUtil.exe.mui', 'CertUtil.exe'],
];
---

<Base title="Gotchas" description="Why OriginalFilename lookups go wrong: the MUI trap, .NET apphosts, values that change across builds, binaries without one, and case sensitivity per SIEM.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Gotchas</h1>
    <p class="lede">Where OriginalFilename detections break, measured on {entries.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} binaries.</p>

    <h2 id="mui">1. Explorer and PowerShell show you the wrong value</h2>
    <p>
      The file Properties dialog, <code>(Get-Item x).VersionInfo</code> and .NET <code>FileVersionInfo</code> load the
      <em>localized</em> MUI copy of the version resource. Sysmon and EDR sensors read the binary's own resource. If you
      copy the value from Explorer into a rule, the rule never fires.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="grid">
        <thead><tr><th>Binary</th><th>Explorer / .NET VersionInfo</th><th>What Sysmon logs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {mui.map(([n, wrong, right]) => (
            <tr><td><a href={href(`b/${n}/`)}>{n}</a></td><td class="mono bad">{wrong}</td><td class="mono good">{right}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Every value in this library comes from parsing <code>RT_VERSION</code> straight out of the PE bytes, never from those APIs.</p>

    <h2 id="apphost">2. Some executables report a DLL name</h2>
    <p>
      An <code>.exe</code> process can log an OriginalFilename ending in <code>.dll</code>. One cause is the .NET (Core) apphost: a
      small native launcher, <code>name.exe</code>, that loads <code>name.dll</code> and carries a copy of the DLL's version
      resource, so <a href={href('b/pwsh.exe/')}><code>pwsh.exe</code></a> logs <code>pwsh.dll</code>. Most of the others are
      ordinary native Windows binaries whose version resource simply names a DLL, such as <code>w32tm.exe</code> →
      <code>w32time.dll</code>. Either way, a rule that expects an <code>.exe</code> value misses them.
    </p>
    <ul class="cols">
      {dllNamed.map((e) => (
        <li>
          <a href={href(`b/${e.slug}/`)} set:html={wbr(e.name)} /> → <code set:html={wbr(dllOf(e))} />
          {e.flags.includes('apphost') && <span class="muted"> (.NET apphost)</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="drift">3. The value changes between builds</h2>
    <p>
      A single value per binary is the common case, but not universal. These {drift.length} binaries changed OriginalFilename,
      or its casing, between Windows releases or architectures. A rule listing one value silently misses the other builds.
    </p>
    <ul class="stack">
      {drift.map((e) => <li><a href={href(`b/${e.slug}/`)} class="mono" set:html={wbr(e.name)} />: <span set:html={md(driftGotcha(e))} /></li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="none">4. Some binaries have no OriginalFilename at all</h2>
    <p>
      Either the version resource is missing, or it has no <code>OriginalFilename</code> key, or the key is empty
      (<a href={href('b/pcalua.exe/')}><code>pcalua.exe</code></a>). Sysmon logs <code>-</code> for all three. OriginalFileName
      rules can't see these binaries; use the image path, hash or signer.
    </p>
    <ul class="cols">
      {noOfn.map((e) => <li><a href={href(`b/${e.slug}/`)} set:html={wbr(e.name)} /></li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="differs">5. The value often isn't the file name</h2>
    <p>
      {byFlag('differs-from-name').length} binaries carry an OriginalFilename that differs from their file name (ignoring case),
      so <code>OriginalFileName: &lt;file name&gt;</code> never matches. Most-referenced in Sigma first:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="grid">
        <thead><tr><th>File name</th><th>OriginalFilename</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {differs.slice(0, 40).map((e) => (
            <tr><td><a href={href(`b/${e.slug}/`)} set:html={wbr(e.name)} /></td><td class="mono" set:html={wbr(e.distinct_values.join(', '))} /></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The <a href={href('')}>library</a>'s "Differs from file name" filter shows the full list.</p>

    <h2 id="case">6. Case sensitivity depends on the backend</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="grid">
        <thead><tr><th>Backend</th><th>Case-insensitive</th><th>Case-sensitive</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sigma</td><td>default string matching</td><td><code>|cased</code> modifier</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Splunk</td><td><code>field=value</code> in search, <code>IN</code></td><td><code>where</code> / <code>eval</code> comparisons, CSV lookups by default</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Defender KQL</td><td><code>=~</code>, <code>in~</code></td><td><code>==</code>, <code>in</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Elastic</td><td>EQL <code>:</code>, <code>in~</code></td><td>Kibana KQL on keyword fields, EQL <code>==</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cribl Stream</td><td>lowercase both sides</td><td>JavaScript <code>===</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The snippets on every page use the case-insensitive form, and exports are lowercased where they feed lookups.</p>

    <h2 id="spoof">7. OriginalFilename is attacker-controlled</h2>
    <p>
      It survives renaming, which is why it's useful, but it's just a string in a resource section. Anyone can set it on their own
      tool, or edit it on a copied binary. Editing a signed binary breaks its signature: Authenticode covers the resources, and
      catalog-signed Windows files stop matching their catalog hash. Pair OriginalFileName with signature status and signer for
      anything that matters.
    </p>

    <h2 id="names">8. One file name, different binaries</h2>
    <p>
      Generic names like <a href={href('b/setup.exe/')}><code>setup.exe</code></a> cover unrelated binaries that happen to share a
      name. Check the path and signer before trusting a lookup on the name alone.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sigma-orphans">9. Sigma literals this database can't vouch for</h2>
    <p>
      SigmaHQ ({stats.sigma_release}) rules match {sigmaOrphans.length} <code>OriginalFileName</code> literals that no binary here
      is named and no parsed build carries. Each one is either a binary this library hasn't collected yet (DLLs loaded by
      image_load rules, third-party tools) — or a rule arm that can never fire. Until the binary is parsed, the value is an
      unverified claim.
    </p>
    <ul class="cols">
      {sigmaOrphans.map((s) => (
        <li><code set:html={wbr(s.value)} /> <span class="muted">({s.rules} rule{s.rules > 1 ? 's' : ''})</span></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
